INFERENCE WEEKLY
Friday, March 13, 2026

Hello. The week in running models, for people who pay the bill.

THE OUTAGE: what the post-mortem said
Tuesday's four-hour incident was a configuration push to the request-routing layer that sent a majority of traffic to one region's capacity. The provider is offering service credits to affected customers; the credits are calculated against committed spend, so the pay-as-you-go customers get nothing. Twenty-two readers sent us their experience. The ones with a tested fallback lost minutes; the ones without lost the afternoon. The median cost of running on the fallback provider for four hours was 1.4x normal.

AGENT TOKEN MATH
Following up on a point several of you made: agentic workloads produce far more output tokens per request than chat, and output tokens cost 4-5x input. We collected traces from eleven agent deployments. Median input-to-output ratio: 2.8 to 1. Median cost per task: $0.31, of which 68% was output tokens. If you are quoting customers a per-task price, this is the number to model.

SMALL MODELS ON CPUS
Compute Ledger ran the numbers this week and we independently confirm them: 1-4B models on ordinary cloud CPUs handle classification and routing at a few cents per million tokens. We moved our own newsletter's spam filter to one and it is fine.

FIVE LINKS
1. The provider's post-mortem, which is better than most.
2. A fallback-routing configuration guide for the two most common gateways.
3. The ASIC vendor's benchmark disclosure, with the caveats in the footnotes.
4. A paper on why quantized small models fail on long inputs, and where the cliff is.
5. A very good thread on rate-limit strategy for bursty agent traffic.

ONE NUMBER: 68%. Share of agent task cost that is output tokens, in our sample.

That's the week. Reply with what you are running.

